```cpp
// learn/parse.h
#pragma once
#include <string>
namespace learn {
    int parse(const std::string& text);        // learn::parse.
}

// json/parse.h (another library)
namespace json {
    int parse(const std::string& text);        // json::parse.
}

// main.cpp
#include <iostream>
#include "learn/parse.h"
#include "json/parse.h"

int main() {
    using std::cout;                           // one name, one scope.
    cout << learn::parse("42") << '\n';        // explicit: no ambiguity.
    cout << json::parse("{}") << '\n';
    // using namespace learn; using namespace json;
    // parse("x");   error: ambiguous.
}
```

| Form | Brings in | Where OK |
|---|---|---|
| `learn::parse(...)`. | nothing: names it directly. | anywhere. |
| `using std::cout;`. | one name. | .cpp files, function scope. |
| `using namespace std;`. | every name. | small .cpp files at most; never headers. |

```reveal
{"prompt": "Why can a using-directive in a header break files that don't use the library?", "answer": "Headers are pasted into every includer. The directive comes along, so every file now sees all those names, and any of its own names can suddenly clash."}
